 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can reduce samples based on allocated render time [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the total size of the 3D textures for all volumes, limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** Performance depends on GPU hardware, resolution, and scene complexity. `ReportProgress` can significantly slow down rendering of small datasets due to `glFinish()` blocking [S2, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Supports up to 5 volumes (1 primary + 4 additional). Scaling to more volumes is limited by `MAX_NUMBER_OF_ADDITIONAL_VOLUMES` [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes, hardware acceleration via shaders, adaptive sampling, masking support [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to 5 volumes, requires GPU with shader support, non-zero extents require workaround, silent failure on invalid cropping [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When multiple volumes need to be rendered simultaneously with accurate compositing and shading [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When more than 5 volumes are needed, or when GPU hardware lacks required extensions [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Each sample requires texture lookups and compositing for each volume [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the total size of the 3D textures for all volumes, plus overhead for transform matrices and clipping planes. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance**: Performance depends on GPU hardware, resolution, and scene complexity. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can improve performance by reducing sample count in empty or uniform regions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling**: Scales with the number of volumes, but each additional volume adds texture lookups and compositing steps per sample [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Hardware-accelerated, supports multiple volumes with different coordinate systems, adaptive sampling, masking [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Limited by GPU memory and loop iteration limits, requires OpenGL support, complex setup for multiple volumes [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use**: When multiple volumes need to be rendered simultaneously with different transfer functions and coordinate systems, and hardware acceleration is available [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use**: When only a single volume is rendered (use `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper`), or when GPU memory is insufficient for the combined volume data [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to enable multi-volume rendering on the GPU [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` and uses GLSL shaders for ray traversal and compositing, chosen for their speed and broad device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. The project includes a demo for testing purposes and modifies several VTK classes to handle multiple inputs and transforms [S1, unofficial].
+The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to add support for multiple input datasets and properties [S1, unofficial]. The implementation uses GLSL shaders instead of OpenCL for general GPU programming because shaders are faster and supported by more devices [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` and integrates with VTK's volume rendering pipeline [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
